d_alternatives": ["B1", "B3"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=67%, source=both]\n\nEndocrine/Bone Disorder (Hyperparathyroidism) vs Malignancy with Hypercalcemia:\n  Favours first:  abnormal cortical bone morphology, abnormal endocardium morphology, abnormal form of the vertebral bodies, abnormal hip bone morphology, abnormality of cardiovascular system morphology\n  Favours second: 2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal obesity, abnormal cardiac septum morphology, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology\n\nRelated to Endocrine/Bone Disorder (Hyperparathyroidism): brittle bone syndrome lethal type, osteogenesis imperfecta\n\nRelated to Malignancy with Hypercalcemia: hypercalcemia disease, paraneoplastic syndrome\n\n[RAG context for 'A 45-year-old African American woman presents to her primary care physician for not feeling well' (score=0.69):]\n  Source: Update on multiple endocrine neoplasia Type 1 and 2. > History and Physical\n  It is also important to assess patients for hypercalcemia symptoms due to primary hyperparathyroidism and its associated clinical complications (eg, renal calculi and osteoporosis); signs and symptoms\n[RAG context for 'A 45-year-old African American woman presents to her primary care physician for not feeling well' (score=0.70):]\n  Source: Hyperparathyroidism. > Differential Diagnosis\n  Malignancy-Related Hypercalcemia\n[RAG context for 'She states she has had a cough for the past week' (score=0.83):]\n  Source: Hypercalcemia of Malignancy. > Introduction\n  More than 90% of hypercalcemia cases are caused by primary hyperparathyroidism (PHPT) or malignancy-induced hypercalcemia, with malignancy being the most common cause of hypercalcemia in hospitalized \n[RAG context for 'She states she has had a cough for the past week' (score=0.83):]\n  Source: Hypercalcemia of Malignancy. > History and Physical\n  Musculoskeletal symptoms may present as muscle weakness and bone pain. Cardiovascular manifestations are subtle, often including short QTc intervals, with rare cases of more severe arrhythmias. Excess\n[Indirect reasoning chains (clinical inference):]\n  history of GERD with antacid use and poorly controlled symptoms → antacid overuse-induced hypercalcemia → Reactive/Non-malignant Hypercalcemia (Antacid Overuse or Infection)\n    (frequency: occasional)\n    Suggestion: Evaluate serum calcium levels and antacid usage patterns to confirm antacid-induced hypercalcemia as the underlying mechanism\n  abdominal pain and trouble focusing with weight loss → metabolic derangement due to hypercalcemia → Malignancy with Hypercalcemia\n    (frequency: frequent)\n    Suggestion: Order calcium, PTH, and tumor markers to differentiate between reactive hypercalcemia and malignancy-related hypercalcemia\n\nWhen indirect_reasoning_chains are present, generate candidates that investigate the INTERMEDIATE phenotype to confirm or rule out the indirect association."}
